.TH TABASM 1 "September 2026" "tabasm 2026.9.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
tabasm \- table-driven cross-assembler for 8- and 16-bit microprocessors
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B tabasm
.BI \-\-cpu " name"
.RI [ options ]
.I source
.RI [ object
.RI [ listing
.RI [ export
.RI [ symbol ]]]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Two-pass absolute assembler.
The instruction set is not compiled in: it is read at run time from a table
file, one per processor.
Adding a target means writing a table, not changing the program.
.PP
Only
.I source
is required.
The other names default to the source base name \(em the source name truncated
at its first dot, not its last \(em with
.BR .obj ", " .lst ", " .exp " and " .sym .
.PP
Output is byte-compatible with the Telemark Assembler.
See
.B COMPATIBILITY
below.
.SH OPTIONS
Option letters are case-insensitive, except
.BR \-y .
Values shown as
.I xx
are hexadecimal.
Options and file names may be interleaved.
.TP
.BI \-\-cpu " name"
Select the instruction table.
.I name
is the table's file name without extension: \fB\-\-cpu z80\fR reads
.IR z80.tab2 .
Required.
.TP
.BI \-a xx
Strict-check mask.
Bare
.B \-a
sets all bits.
Default 06.
.TP
.B \-b
Binary object output and block mode.
Equivalent to
.BR "\-g3 \-c" .
.TP
.B \-c
Block mode.
A counter change does not split the object into separate records.
.TP
.BI \-d " name"
Define
.IR name ,
as
.B #define
would.
.TP
.B \-e
List macro lines in expanded form rather than as written.
.TP
.BI \-f xx
Fill byte for locations the source never writes.
.TP
.BI \-g n
Object format.
0 Intel HEX, 1 MOS Technology, 2 Motorola S-record, 3 raw binary,
4 Intel HEX with word addresses.
Default 0.
.TP
.B \-h
Append a hex dump to the listing.
.TP
.B \-i
Fold case in symbol names.
.TP
.B \-l
Append the label table.
.B \-ll
prints the long form,
.B \-la
prints all labels.
.TP
.B \-m
MOS Technology object format.
Same as
.BR \-g1 .
.TP
.BI \-o xx
Bytes per object record.
Default 18 (24 decimal).
.TP
.BI \-p n
Page length in lines, decimal.
Pages the listing.
.TP
.B \-q
Suppress the listing.
.TP
.B \-s
Write the symbol file.
.TP
.BI \-x d
Instruction class mask, one hex digit.
Bare
.B \-x
enables all classes.
Default 1.
.TP
.B \-y
Print elapsed time and line count.
.TP
.B \-z
Write a trace to standard error.
Its content is not a stable interface.
.TP
.B \-\-compatibility
Restore three behaviours of the original: expressions evaluate left to right
with no operator precedence,
.B .UNDEF
does nothing, and a
.B %
with no binary digit after it is absorbed, the value following it being taken.
.TP
.B \-\-bug\-compatibility
Restore three defects the original had: the
.B \-g4
checksum is computed from the byte address while the record prints the word
address, the symbol list is sorted by an algorithm that stops early, and a byte
emitted past
.B 0xFFFF
draws no diagnostic.
.TP
.B \-\-help
Print the options and exit.
There is no
.B \-h
for this:
.B \-h
appends a hex dump to the listing.
.TP
.BI \-\-message\-prefix " name"
Name on the assembler's own messages.
Default
.BR tabasm .
.TP
.BI \-\-page\-title " text"
Heading for a paged listing when the source sets none.
Default
.BR tabasm .
.SH TABLES
A table is looked for in these places, first hit winning:
.IP 1. 4
.BR $TASMTABS ,
which names one directory.
.IP 2. 4
The working directory.
.IP 3. 4
.I tables/
beside the executable.
.IP 4. 4
.I ../share/tabasm/tables
relative to the executable.
.I /usr/local/bin/tabasm
therefore finds
.IR /usr/local/share/tabasm/tables .
.IP 5. 4
.IR $XDG_DATA_HOME/tabasm/tables ,
or
.I ~/.local/share/tabasm/tables
when that is unset.
.IP 6. 4
.IR /usr/local/share/tabasm/tables ,
then
.IR /usr/share/tabasm/tables .
.PP
Two file names are tried in each directory:
.IB name .tab2
first, then
.IB tasm name .tab
for a table in the older format.
If none is found, the error lists every path tried.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TASMTABS
One directory to search for tables.
Not a path list.
.TP
.B TASMOPTS
Options appended to the command line.
.TP
.B TASMERRFORMAT
Format for diagnostics.
Takes four conversions in this order: %s file, %d line, %s message, %s detail.
Anything else is rejected with a warning and the built-in layout is used,
which is equivalent to
.BR "%s line %04d: %s %s" .
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
No errors.
.TP
.B 1
Assembly completed with errors.
.TP
.B 2
Out of memory.
.TP
.B 3
A file could not be opened.
.TP
.B 4
Fatal internal error.
.SH COMPATIBILITY
tabasm is an independent reimplementation of the Telemark Assembler (TASM),
first released in 1985.
Its output matches the 2001 release byte for byte across 363 compared
artefacts.
.PP
Several of the original's defects are corrected by default;
.B \-\-bug\-compatibility
restores them.
Where its evaluation semantics changed between versions,
.B \-\-compatibility
selects the older.
To reproduce the original exactly:
.PP
.RS
.EX
tabasm \-\-compatibility \-\-bug\-compatibility \-\-message\-prefix tasm \\
       \-\-cpu 8051 prog.asm
.EE
.RE
.PP
The legacy
.BI \- nn
and
.BI \-t name
table selectors still work, undocumented.
.SH EXAMPLES
Assemble for the Z80, Motorola S-record output:
.PP
.RS
.EX
tabasm \-\-cpu z80 \-g2 firmware.asm firmware.s19
.EE
.RE
.PP
Raw binary, filling gaps with FF, with a label table:
.PP
.RS
.EX
tabasm \-\-cpu 6303 \-b \-fff \-l firmware.asm firmware.bin
.EE
.RE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I *.tab2
Instruction table.
.TP
.I *.tab
Instruction table, older format.
.SH SEE ALSO
.I table-format.md
describes the table format,
.I table-format-legacy.md
the older one.
.PP
.B tab1to2
rewrites a legacy table in the current format.
It is built from the source repository and is not part of the binary release;
the assembler reads legacy tables as they are.
